.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt LD 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm ld
.Nd Substrate ELF linker for i386 and x86-64
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl m32 | Fl m64 | Fl m Ar emulation
.Op Fl r | Fl shared | Fl pie | Fl static
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Fl e Ar symbol
.Op Fl -dynamic-linker Ar path
.Op Fl soname Ar name
.Op Fl rpath Ar dir
.Op Fl L Ar dir
.Op Fl l Ar name
.Op Fl Bstatic | Fl Bdynamic
.Op Fl -start-group ... Fl -end-group
.Op Fl -whole-archive | Fl -no-whole-archive
.Op Fl -as-needed | Fl -no-as-needed
.Op Fl T Ar script
.Op Fl -gc-sections
.Op Fl -print-gc-sections
.Op Fl -icf Ns = Ns Ar safe|all|none
.Op Fl -hash-style Ns = Ns Ar sysv|gnu|both
.Op Fl -allow-undefined | Fl -no-undefined
.Op Fl -unresolved-symbols Ns = Ns Ar ignore-all|report-all
.Op Fl -undefined Ar symbol
.Op Fl -defsym Ar name=value
.Op Fl -trace
.Op Fl -trace-symbol Ns = Ns Ar symbol
.Op Fl -warn-common
.Op Fl -fatal-warnings
.Op Fl -z Ar text|notext|execstack|noexecstack|relro|norelro|now|lazy
.Op Fl -plugin Ar path
.Op Fl -plugin-opt Ar option
.Op Fl -Map Ar file
.Op Fl -reproduce Ar dir
.Op Fl -compat Ns = Ns Ar gnu|lld
.Op Fl -version
.Op Fl -help
.Ar input ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the native Substrate linker implementation for ELF inputs.
It links
.Dv ET_REL
objects and archives into relocatable, executable, and shared outputs.
It does not forward to external linker backends.
.Pp
When no target mode is forced,
.Nm
auto-selects i386 or x86-64 mode from the first compatible input.
.Pp
When installed through the host toolchain workflow described in
.Xr host_toolchain 7 ,
the installed
.Xr cc 1
driver resolves the sibling linker from the same prefix before falling back to
other tools on
.Ev PATH .
.Pp
For
.Fl l
resolution,
.Nm
searches
.Fl L
paths first, then host defaults
.Pa /usr/lib
and
.Pa /usr/local/lib .
The
.Fl L
directories are searched in the order given, and each for
.Pa libNAME.so
and then
.Pa libNAME.a ;
a library that is for another machine than the link is passed over.
The machine is the one
.Fl m
names, or else that of the first input that is of one; looking into
libraries later does not change it.
Both regular ELF libraries and GNU ld script wrappers
.Pq Dv INPUT / Dv GROUP
are supported.
.Pp
An input is an archive if it begins as one does, whatever it is called;
ordinary and thin archives are read.
An archive that is truncated or whose member headers do not make sense is
an error that says what is wrong and at what offset, and ends the link.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--unresolved-symbols=report-all"
.It Fl m32 , Fl m64 , Fl m Ar emulation
Select output architecture mode.
The emulations are
.Cm elf_i386
and
.Cm elf_x86_64 ,
each also under the name the Substrate GCC gives it,
.Cm elf_i386_substrate
and
.Cm elf_x86_64_substrate .
.It Fl -eh-frame-hdr
Make
.Pa .eh_frame_hdr ,
the sorted table of functions and their unwinding records that an
unwinder searches, and the
.Dv PT_GNU_EH_FRAME
header that points to it.
A C++ program that is dynamically linked cannot catch an exception
without it.
Records for functions that were left out of the output have no entry;
if the records are in a form the linker does not read, the section is
made with no table, which sends the unwinder to
.Pa .eh_frame
itself.
.It Fl -sysroot Ns = Ns Ar dir
Accepted; the libraries are found through the
.Fl L
directories the compiler driver gives.
.It Fl -copy-dt-needed-entries , Fl -no-copy-dt-needed-entries
With the first, a shared library that a named shared library needs is
looked for as
.Fl l : Ns Ar name
would be and, if it defines something the link still wants, is used and
recorded as needed too, and so on through what it needs.
This is how a C++ program linked with
.Pa libstdc++
finds the unwinder in
.Pa libgcc_s .
The default is not to.
.It Fl r
Produce relocatable
.Dv ET_REL
output.
.It Fl shared
Produce a shared object: no entry is needed, what it leaves undefined is
for the program that loads it to supply, and it is not made executable.
.It Fl pie
Produce a position-independent executable.
It is
.Dv ET_DYN
as a shared object is, and a program in every other way: it begins at
its entry, an undefined symbol is an error, it is made executable, and
.Dv DF_1_PIE
marks it.
.Fl no-pie
undoes it.
.It Fl static
Use archives for the libraries that follow.
It does not change what is produced:
.Fl static Fl pie
is a PIE.
.Pp
Executables and shared objects are branded for Substrate
.Pq Dv ELFOSABI_SUBSTRATE ,
whichever system the linker itself runs on; relocatable output is not.
.It Fl e Ar symbol
Where an executable begins.
A symbol named here, or by
.Ic ENTRY
in a script, that is not defined is an error.
Without either the program begins at
.Li _start ,
and if there is none at the start of
.Pa .text ,
with a warning that says so.
.It Fl -dynamic-linker Ar path
The program interpreter of the output
.Pq Dv PT_INTERP ;
on Substrate
.Pa /sbin/ld.so .
.It Fl soname Ar name , Fl h Ar name
The name a shared object gives itself
.Pq Dv DT_SONAME ,
which is the name programs linked against it will ask for.
.It Fl rpath Ar dir
A directory for the dynamic linker to search, recorded in
.Dv DT_RUNPATH ;
repeated, the directories are joined with colons.
Substrate's dynamic linker does not consult it.
.Fl rpath-link
is accepted and has no effect.
.Pp
These three may be written with one dash or two, and with their value as
the next argument or after
.Ql = .
.It Fl L Ar dir , Fl l Ar name
Add a library search directory and request a library.
.It Fl Bstatic , Fl Bdynamic
Toggle static/dynamic library lookup mode for subsequent
.Fl l
operands.
.It Fl -start-group ... Fl -end-group
Repeatedly scan grouped libraries until no new unresolved symbols are satisfied.
.It Fl -whole-archive , Fl -no-whole-archive
Force extraction of all archive members for subsequent
.Fl l
or archive inputs.
.It Fl -as-needed , Fl -no-as-needed
Control whether DSOs without currently unresolved symbol matches are ignored.
.It Fl T Ar script
Use a linker script; see
.Sx LINKER SCRIPTS .
.It Fl Ttext-segment Ar address
Where the image begins: the address of the first loaded segment, a
multiple of the page size.
The default is 0x08048000 for i386 and 0x400000 for x86-64, and 0 for a
shared object.
.Fl Ttext ,
.Fl Tdata
and
.Fl Tbss
are refused; a linker script says where a section goes.
.It Fl S , Fl -strip-debug , Fl s , Fl -strip-all
Leave the debugging information out of the output.
.Fl s
does no more than
.Fl S :
the symbol table is kept.
.It Fl q , Fl -emit-relocs
Keep the relocation sections in an executable or shared object.
.It Fl -build-id
Accepted, in either form; no build ID is made.
.It Fl -gc-sections
Enable section garbage collection.
.It Fl -print-gc-sections
Print removed sections while doing
.Fl -gc-sections .
.It Fl -icf Ns = Ns Ar mode
Enable identical code/data folding in
.Ar safe
or
.Ar all
mode.
.It Fl -hash-style Ns = Ns Ar style
Select dynamic symbol hash style.
.It Fl -defsym Ar name=value
Define or override an absolute symbol.
.It Fl -undefined Ar symbol
Force a symbol into the unresolved root set.
.It Fl -allow-undefined , Fl -no-undefined
Set unresolved symbol policy.
.It Fl -unresolved-symbols Ns = Ns Ar policy
GNU-style unresolved symbol policy alias.
.It Fl -trace , Fl -trace-symbol Ns = Ns Ar symbol
Trace input loading and symbol usage.
.It Fl -z Ar option
Set text-relocation, stack-execute, and RELRO policy.
With
.Cm relro ,
the default, what only the dynamic linker writes
.Po
.Pa .data.rel.ro ,
the constructor tables,
.Pa .dynamic ,
.Pa .got
.Pc
is laid out together on pages of its own, ahead of the data the program
writes, and
.Dv PT_GNU_RELRO
covers those pages whole.
Substrate's dynamic linker makes them read-only in the libraries it
loads, not in the program the kernel loaded.
.Cm now
marks the output for the dynamic linker to bind every symbol when it is
loaded
.Pq Dv DT_BIND_NOW , DF_BIND_NOW , DF_1_NOW ;
.Cm lazy
undoes it.
Any other keyword is a warning and is ignored.
.It Fl -plugin Ar path
Set plugin executable used for LTO materialization.
A plugin that is a shared object, which is what GCC names on every link,
cannot be run and is set aside; an input that then turns out to hold LTO
bytecode is an error.
.It Fl -plugin-opt Ar option
Pass plugin-specific options.
.It Fl -Map Ar file
Write a link map including inputs, sections, program headers, and symbol provenance.
.It Fl -reproduce Ar dir
Write a reproducible link bundle containing object snapshots, manifest, and replay script.
.It Fl -compat Ns = Ns Ar gnu|lld
Set unsupported-option policy for compatibility behavior.
.El
.Sh SECTIONS
With no linker script an input section goes to the output section of its
own name, except that a section named
.Pa .text.NAME ,
.Pa .rodata.NAME ,
.Pa .data.rel.ro.NAME ,
.Pa .data.NAME ,
.Pa .bss.NAME ,
.Pa .tdata.NAME ,
.Pa .tbss.NAME
or
.Pa .gcc_except_table.NAME
goes to the section named without the
.Pa .NAME .
The constructor tables
.Pq Pa .init_array.N
are not gathered, and with
.Fl r
nothing is.
Where sections of strings or constants
.Pq Dv SHF_MERGE
are gathered with others the result is not marked as such.
.Sh LINKER SCRIPTS
The script named by
.Fl T
is read once, before anything is linked, and a script that cannot be read
is reported with its file, line and column and ends the link.
The commands are those of GNU ld.
Understood and acted on:
.Bl -tag -width "INCLUDE file"
.It Ic SECTIONS
Output section descriptions, each
.Dq name [address] : [ALIGN(n)] { ... } [>region] [:phdr ...] ,
and between them assignments,
.Ic PROVIDE ,
.Ic ASSERT
and
.Ic INCLUDE .
An input section goes to the first output section whose body asks for it
by file and section pattern, with the wildcards
.Ql * ,
.Ql \&?
and
.Ql [...] ;
.Ic EXCLUDE_FILE
is honoured,
.Ic KEEP
protects the section from
.Fl -gc-sections ,
and
.Ic /DISCARD/
leaves what it names out.
An input section nothing asks for keeps its own name, and is placed where
the default layout would have put it among the sections the script names.
.It Ic PHDRS
The program headers of the output.
A section goes in the headers named after its output section, and one
that names none goes where the one before it went.
A
.Dv PT_LOAD
without
.Ic FLAGS
has the permissions of the sections in it.
.It Ic MEMORY
Regions that
.Dq >region
places sections in; a section that does not fit is an error.
.It Ic ENTRY
The entry symbol, unless
.Fl e
names one.
.It Ic INCLUDE
Another script, read where the command stands as if its text stood there.
The file is looked for beside the including script, then as named, then
in the
.Fl L
directories.
.It Ic ASSERT
An error with the given message when its expression is zero.
The semicolon after it is optional.
.El
.Pp
Inside
.Ic SECTIONS
the location counter
.Ql \&.
is the address the next thing is placed at.
It begins where the default layout begins the image and may only move
forwards.
Assigned between output sections it places what follows; in a body it may
stand before the first input section description, where it is the
section's start, or after the last, where moving it makes the section
longer.
A statement between two input section descriptions is refused: where the
pieces of an output section meet is not kept.
.Pp
A symbol is defined by
.Dq name = expression ;
or changed by
.Dq name += expression ;
and the other compound operators.
.Ic PROVIDE
defines its symbol only if an input refers to it and none defines it;
.Ic HIDDEN
and
.Ic PROVIDE_HIDDEN
make the symbol hidden.
A symbol defined inside
.Ic SECTIONS
belongs to the section its address falls in; one defined outside is
absolute.
Expressions have the operators of C, parentheses nested no more than 256
deep, and the functions
.Ic ALIGN
.Po
with one argument the location counter rounded up to a multiple of it,
with two the first rounded up to a multiple of the second
.Pc ,
.Ic ADDR ,
.Ic LOADADDR ,
.Ic SIZEOF ,
.Ic ALIGNOF ,
.Ic DEFINED ,
.Ic MAX ,
.Ic MIN ,
.Ic ABSOLUTE ,
.Ic ORIGIN
and
.Ic LENGTH
of a memory region,
.Ic CONSTANT
of
.Dv MAXPAGESIZE
or
.Dv COMMONPAGESIZE
.Pq both 4096 ,
.Ic SIZEOF_HEADERS ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_ALIGN ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_END ,
.Ic DATA_SEGMENT_RELRO_END
and
.Ic SEGMENT_START
.Pq always its default .
Any other function is an error that names it.
A number may end in
.Ql K
or
.Ql M .
Of
.Dq c \&? a : b
only the arm that is taken is evaluated, so that
.Dq DEFINED(x) \&? x : 0
is not an error when
.Ar x
is undefined.
A name in an expression is a symbol the script defines or one of the
program's, whose value is its address; a name that is neither is an
error.
.Ic DEFINED
is true of both kinds.
A statement may use a symbol that a later statement of the script
defines.
.Pp
Read and not acted on:
.Ic OUTPUT_FORMAT ,
.Ic OUTPUT_ARCH ,
.Ic OUTPUT ,
.Ic TARGET ,
.Ic SEARCH_DIR ,
.Ic INPUT ,
.Ic GROUP ,
.Ic STARTUP ,
.Ic EXTERN ,
.Ic VERSION ,
.Ic INSERT ,
.Ic NOCROSSREFS ,
.Ic REGION_ALIAS ,
the load address
.Pq Ic AT ,
.Ic SUBALIGN ,
.Ic FILL
and the fill value of an output section, and the sorting of
.Ic SORT .
Refused:
.Ic BYTE ,
.Ic SHORT ,
.Ic LONG
and
.Ic QUAD .
.Sh OUTPUT
The output is written beside its name and moved into place when it is
whole, so that an existing file of that name is replaced at once or not
at all.
A name that is not a plain file is written into instead: a link is
followed, and a device or a pipe receives the output and stays what it
was.
A file the linker makes has the mode a new file has, less the umask, and
is executable if it is a program.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics are emitted in parser-friendly one-line forms such as
.Dq ld: error: ...
and
.Dq ld: warning: ... .
For select classes,
.Nm
adds structured notes with category/source/hint metadata.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY NOTES
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm
implements GNU-compatible options for the supported feature set but is not a full GNU ld script superset;
.Sx LINKER SCRIPTS
says what of a script is acted on.
.It
Input sections stored compressed
.Pq Dv SHF_COMPRESSED , zlib
are linked as what they hold and written uncompressed.
Sections that are not loaded, the debugging information among them, are
relocated like the rest, and an executable or shared object carries no
relocation sections of the linker's own.
.It
A shared library is recorded as needed under its own
.Dv DT_SONAME ,
not under the name it was found by.
.It
In an executable, a weak reference that neither an input nor a shared
object of the link defines is zero at link time.
.It
An executable's dynamic symbol table has what it imports, what the
shared objects of the link refer to and it defines, its copies of their
variables, and with
.Fl -export-dynamic
everything global.
.It
A shared object binds references to its own global symbols when it is
linked, as GNU ld does under
.Fl Bsymbolic :
a definition of the same name in the program does not take their place.
.It
In an executable made of code that is not position-independent, a call
to a function in a shared object goes through the PLT; a function whose
address is taken has its PLT entry for an address, the same in every
module; and a variable of a shared object gets a copy in the executable
.Pq Pa .dynbss , Dv R_*_COPY ,
under every name the shared object has for it.
A thread-local variable referred to that way is an error.
.It
.Fl z Cm text
refuses an output in which the dynamic linker would have to apply a
relocation to read-only memory, which is what a shared object made of
code that is not position-independent comes to.
Without it such an output is marked
.Dv DT_TEXTREL
and a warning says so;
.Fl z Cm notext
silences the warning.
Substrate's dynamic linker does not apply relocations to read-only
memory.
.It
GNU plugin shared-object API is not loaded directly;
.Fl -plugin
expects an executable materializer interface.
.It
Behavior for unsupported options differs by
.Fl -compat
mode.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr as 1 ,
.Xr cc 1 ,
.Xr readelf 1 ,
.Xr elfobj 3 ,
.Xr host_toolchain 7
